we got a chance to speak to dodger hall  of famer vin scully  yesterday after the show uh hunt  auctions is honoring uh the  great vince scully they're going to have  an auction and it's going to take place  coming up in september  and you're gonna you're gonna be able to  bid on some items here  his world series ring from 1988  you're also going to be able to bid on  the scorebook  in his last game that he did another  world series ring here uh president  reagan's  uh signed baseball to vin scully is also  in there  and i started out the conversation with  vince golly by asking him  how his summer was my summer  has been hide and seek the temperatures  out here  have been unusually high where our  backyard temperature one day  was 117. however  my wife and i spend most of our lives  now  at home so i could just sum up the  summer in two words  at home do you miss watching games or  calling games  you know i don't miss calling them at  all  and i must say i kind of demand  something  from the game i'm watching if it's a  very slowly played game  if it's just a long game i might just  turn it off  and go to bed we go to bed reasonably  early  so i like to watch him for a while  and right now it's for instance today  or this will be on the air tomorrow  friday but  today while we're taping this we have  football coming on tonight we have the  dodgers playing tonight we have a  basketball game tonight  so we spend our time my wife and i  bouncing from one sport to another  and on the weekend we really look  forward  to watching golf have you ever called a  game without  fans no never  maybe reasonably small crowds going back  to brooklyn  where you could hear the voices  of the individual fans i remember  i had just started with red barber and  connie desmond  and i was really a kid and there was a  famous woman fan her name was hilda  chester and she had a cowbell and she  worked away all over the ballpark she  was  extremely colorful and this particular  day  with a very small crowd i heard her  heavy voice say  then scully i love you  and the crowd whatever there was kind of  laugh and i lowered my head  and she said look at me when i'm talking  to you  that's how small the crowd was one day  in epistles  but how important is that crowd for you  especially when you're doing a game on  radio  oh i loved it i i've told this story  many times  when i grew up about eight years old we  had a big  four-legged radio and i would crawl  underneath it with some saltine crackers  and a glass of milk  and i would listen to a college football  game  and let's just say it was alabama  tennessee  i had no idea who was playing or  anything  but i learned to love the roar of the  crowd  and if i have a trademark or had it  would be  call to play as quickly and accurately  as i could  and then shut up and listen to the crowd  and for a few seconds i was eight years  old again  and someone during one of the world  series games  somebody hit a home run and i called the  home run  and as networks are one to do they  showed the home run again  and i just shut up and the columnist  wrote  perhaps vin scully made his greatest  contribution  by saying nothing and i really i took  that to heart  i didn't think of it as criticism i  thought of as yes you're right  the best thing i can do in the crowd  roars is shut up there's nothing better  but to have that self-discipline because  it feels like announcers want to  over call a game and here you are  having almost a seat belt on to say  plays over  quiet let the crowd or ambience settle  in  and it almost feels like it would draw  me closer  to the game while i'm listening because  i almost lean in more  because you're not saying anything yeah  i think  again it goes all the way back to my  love of the roar of the crowd probably  a couple of the longest pauses  while i had an enjoyment of listening to  the crowd  one would certainly be kirk gibson's  world series home run  and another one was henry aaron's  record-breaking home run  i mean what the heck can you say once  you've called the home run  except sit back and really luxuriate  listening to this crowd just be ecstatic  i loved it but you were also on the call  for the catch with joe  montana and dwight clark did you  react the same way in a football moment  as you would in a baseball moment  oh sure i mean if i remember there was  less than a minute to go  and montana throws to clark and the  catch is made  and the niners go out in front there was  still  some seconds left for dallas to come  back  but when dwight clark caught that i  believe  i said candlestick is a mad house  and i shut up and i just loved it i got  goosebumps and  that was the great part of the job  shutting up  he's uh vin scully the hall of famer the  former  dodger broadcaster and now on twitter  uh what took you so long to get on  twitter vin  well to be honest when i hung it up back  in  2016 i figured i had said  enough for a lifetime but after a while  i realized i didn't really miss doing  the game  but i kind of missed hearing from the  fans a little bit  and some friends of mine said you ought  to get on social media  and maybe you can exchange thoughts with  people  so after listening to some friends for a  couple of years  finally went on it and i don't plan to  do  anything controversial at no time  will i give an opinion i will only try  to deal in facts  and basically baseball facts nothing  else  so it'll be very low key and if  something is on twitter asking me a  question  if i can answer it i'd be more than  happy to do it and basically  that's why just for the fun of it i  guess you could say  vin is also auctioning off some of his  personal collection  and this will take place at september  23rd live  internet auction hunt auctions the  scully collection consists of materials  related to  his 67-year broadcasting career starting  with brooklyn and then the la dodgers  you're going back to the 50s up through  2016.  